\details{
\subsection{Understanding the Results}{

\strong{P-value}: If p < 0.05, the proportion differs significantly from
the test proportion
\itemize{
\item p < 0.001: Very strong evidence the proportion differs
\item p < 0.01: Strong evidence the proportion differs
\item p < 0.05: Moderate evidence the proportion differs
\item p > 0.05: No significant difference from expected proportion
}

\strong{Observed Proportion}: The actual proportion in your data.
Compare this with the test proportion to see the direction of any
difference.

\strong{Confidence Interval}: The range likely to contain the true
population proportion. If the test proportion falls outside this range,
the result is significant.
}

\subsection{When to Use This}{

Use the binomial test when:
\itemize{
\item You want to compare an observed proportion to a known value
\item Your variable has exactly 2 categories
\item You need an exact test (not relying on normal approximation)
\item Sample size is small (where chi-square may not be reliable)
}
}

\subsection{Relationship to Other Tests}{
\itemize{
\item For testing association between two categorical variables:
Use \code{\link{chi_square}()} instead
\item For comparing proportions between groups:
Use chi-square or z-test for proportions
\item For larger samples with normal approximation: results will be very
similar to a one-sample z-test for proportions
}
}

\subsection{Group 1 and one-tailed tests}{

As in SPSS \code{NPAR TESTS /BINOMIAL}, Group 1 is the category of the
first valid case in the data (per group with \code{group_by()}), and
\code{p} is its hypothesized proportion. For \code{p = 0.5} the p-value
is two-tailed. For any other \code{p} SPSS reports a one-tailed p-value
in the direction of the observed proportion ("the proportion of cases in
the first group < p"); \code{binomial_test()} does the same. To test the
other category, put a case of it first or use \code{1 - p}.
}

\subsection{Weighted variants}{

SPSS \code{NPAR TESTS} ignores \code{WEIGHT BY}, so weighted results have
no SPSS reference. The weighted variant is an R-only frequency-weight
extension that reduces exactly to the unweighted test when all weights
equal 1 (enforced by an internal invariance suite); see
\code{vignette("spss-compatibility")} for validation status.
}
